WEBVTT

00:03.450 --> 00:04.923
Melissa, we're good.

00:09.360 --> 00:11.979
We'd like to welcome everyone here today.

00:14.600 --> 00:17.001
Yeah, yeah, zip it.

00:20.120 --> 00:23.493
Welcome everyone. If you want to stay in the council chambers, you need to stop

00:23.509 --> 00:27.023
talking. Okay. All right, we'd like to welcome

00:27.523 --> 00:30.590
everyone. I'm Melissa Clark. I'm filling in as mayor pro tem today. Mayor Gray is

00:30.767 --> 00:34.784
online with us until about 5:30, but he's

00:35.284 --> 00:39.187
asked me to conduct because I'm here in person. This is the work session

00:39.283 --> 00:42.899
for July 21st, 2026, and it is 4:00

00:42.915 --> 00:46.852
PM. And we are going to start with our discussion

00:47.352 --> 00:51.110
and information items. Our first item on our discussion agenda

00:51.736 --> 00:55.632
is an overview of Best Friends Animal Society programs. And this was a

00:55.840 --> 00:59.368
request brought by Councilmember Huish and Councilmember

00:59.432 --> 01:03.071
Whiting. And I'd like to give Councilmember Whiting just a quick second

01:03.520 --> 01:07.112
to talk about why he asked to have this on the agenda. Well,

01:07.128 --> 01:11.104
I think there's numerous reasons, and I'm grateful that you're here. This is awesome,

01:11.457 --> 01:13.733
and we're excited to hear from you. And I don't want to take too much

01:14.233 --> 01:18.175
time explaining it, but I love cats and I love animals, and apparently

01:18.191 --> 01:21.419
Councilmember Huish is on board with enough of that as well. But there's, I think

01:21.919 --> 01:25.100
there's some awesome tools that they can provide to the city to improve

01:25.132 --> 01:28.813
the way we, we care for animals in our community. So awesome.

01:28.861 --> 01:32.167
So we'll invite Kate Hall and Bailey Tolton from

01:32.296 --> 01:36.003
Best Friends Animal Society to come up to the microphone and

01:36.100 --> 01:36.793
present to us.

01:42.051 --> 01:44.080
Okay, awesome. Can you guys hear me okay?

01:44.708 --> 01:47.847
Okay, we're gonna kind of tag team this. So I'm gonna start the first half

01:48.347 --> 01:50.747
and then I'll turn it over to Bailey. But my name is Kate Hall.

01:51.036 --> 01:53.250
I am a campaign strategist with Best Friends.

01:53.827 --> 01:56.908
And thank you so much for having us here today. We're super excited.

01:57.373 --> 02:00.807
Thanks to Councilmember Whiting and Huish for allowing us to

02:01.307 --> 02:05.010
be here. And we met with them previously to discuss some of this. So we're

02:05.510 --> 02:09.439
just super excited. In your packets, you should have received 2 documents from

02:09.471 --> 02:13.947
us, I believe. The first one is an ordinance review.

02:14.525 --> 02:17.798
We do this quite frequently throughout the state of Utah and throughout the country.

02:18.873 --> 02:22.727
This was requested to just see how your city ordinance is doing, if there's

02:23.227 --> 02:26.677
anything you can do better with pets, anything you can do better with community cats,

02:26.741 --> 02:30.113
things like that. And the one thing that we found that we

02:30.613 --> 02:33.854
would recommend is to update to accept

02:33.886 --> 02:37.724
the Community Cat Act. That was a law that went into effect, I think it

02:38.224 --> 02:41.240
was 2011. The legislature passed, but it's an opt-in,

02:41.288 --> 02:43.328
so cities have to add it to their ordinances.

02:44.266 --> 02:48.164
Recently, Cedar City and Salt Lake have added those. And so the

02:48.664 --> 02:52.532
language that you see before you is language that we've copied from Cedar City.

02:54.343 --> 02:57.707
So our legal team drafted that. It should be applicable.

02:58.047 --> 03:00.521
You can put that right in your ordinance if you want to.

03:02.135 --> 03:05.809
So that is the first document, and I'm happy to answer any questions. I'm not—

03:06.246 --> 03:09.419
I don't have a legal background, so I'd be happy to pass

03:09.919 --> 03:12.710
on any questions that I don't have the answers to, to our legal team and

03:13.210 --> 03:16.277
get back to you. The 2nd document that you have in front of you is

03:16.390 --> 03:19.551
this Utah County resources. It just has 4

03:19.599 --> 03:22.824
different ways that we're offering to partner with cities in Utah

03:22.888 --> 03:26.001
County, one being the ordinance review. So you've already done that,

03:26.017 --> 03:29.643
which is great. The 2nd option is to put some

03:29.707 --> 03:33.253
information on your website. American Fork, the city of

03:33.753 --> 03:37.007
American Fork, has done that. And so I'd be happy to share that link with

03:37.507 --> 03:41.163
you guys. You can also just Google American Fork animal control,

03:41.179 --> 03:44.516
and it should pop up. But they've added some resources.

03:44.693 --> 03:48.367
They've added a phone number for us, and it's actually decreasing the workload on

03:48.463 --> 03:52.089
their animal control because we're responding to more of the

03:52.589 --> 03:56.101
complaints about cats that city members might have,

03:56.181 --> 04:00.818
and they're able to prioritize those higher priority asks

04:01.318 --> 04:05.374
from the community. So that's one thing. Another is to do community

04:05.406 --> 04:09.145
canvassing. So if there's areas in your city that, you know, you have

04:09.177 --> 04:12.596
a big colony of cats, And you know that you have some neighbors

04:12.692 --> 04:15.839
that are great and they love the cats, and then some neighbors that might

04:16.339 --> 04:20.335
not love the cats. We can do outreach and we provide free deterrents

04:20.835 --> 04:23.338
for people who don't want the cats in their yard. They work very well.

04:23.627 --> 04:27.159
We can set them up. They're free. And then we can also provide resources for

04:27.659 --> 04:31.029
the people who do like the cats. So we have a clinic here in

04:31.077 --> 04:34.722
Orem, actually, that does spay and neuters for just a dollar. So we

04:34.770 --> 04:38.302
offer those services too. community members. Um,

04:39.144 --> 04:42.544
and then the last thing that we offer to partner is the Community Cat

04:43.044 --> 04:46.671
Program. So that's doing an entire pilot program. Um, it includes

04:47.171 --> 04:50.117
a lot of these other options as well, and I'm gonna let Bailey kind of

04:50.149 --> 04:52.995
talk more about that because she's the community cat expert. So.

04:55.705 --> 04:59.190
Hi, everyone. So my name is Bailey Tolton. I actually live

04:59.690 --> 05:02.621
in Eagle Mountain, um, in City Center. I've lived here since 2020.

05:03.816 --> 05:07.656
I love our city. Um, it's been a great place to raise my kids.

05:08.492 --> 05:12.557
I do work for Best Friends as the community cat program coordinator.

05:13.328 --> 05:17.216
So, um, I'm not sure if you know this, Utah County

05:17.473 --> 05:21.153
is the only county in the whole state of Utah that is

05:21.249 --> 05:24.768
still euthanizing healthy animals in our shelters.

05:25.587 --> 05:29.508
Eagle Mountain's animals go to one of those shelters and

05:31.064 --> 05:34.578
Um, those— not only

05:34.610 --> 05:37.526
are we paying for these animals to go to the shelter, but a lot of

05:38.026 --> 05:41.445
them don't have great outcomes. Eagle Mountain, with us

05:41.816 --> 05:45.979
becoming so big and so many people moving in, we've seen

05:46.011 --> 05:49.642
an increase in how many cats from our city have entered

05:50.142 --> 05:53.483
that shelter. Um, Cedar City is

05:53.532 --> 05:56.598
similar in size to us, and they have started a community cat

05:57.098 --> 06:01.051
program. I did try and get data. from them regarding

06:01.067 --> 06:03.478
how it's going and was unable to do that.

06:04.384 --> 06:08.264
But I did find a study that was published in New Mexico, or yeah,

06:08.505 --> 06:11.904
Albuquerque, New Mexico, at their shelter. And when they

06:12.404 --> 06:16.301
started their community cat program, it reduced

06:16.801 --> 06:17.685
the cat intake by 37%.

06:19.857 --> 06:23.701
Feline euthanasia decreased by 84% by

06:23.717 --> 06:27.285
having this program. And the live release rate

06:27.831 --> 06:31.490
was 47.1%, meaning almost

06:31.507 --> 06:34.428
48% more cats left that shelter alive.

06:35.760 --> 06:39.099
Um, why this matters to me and why I think it should matter to Eagle

06:39.599 --> 06:42.823
Mountain. Um, we all want our tax dollars to be spent

06:43.323 --> 06:46.836
wisely. I know you guys know that, and I know the citizens know that

06:47.336 --> 06:51.395
as well. Um, I think this is a great opportunity for us to be proactive

06:51.651 --> 06:56.398
with our growing city. I think it is a great

06:56.898 --> 07:01.391
opportunity to use our money to have our

07:01.471 --> 07:05.084
animal services money to help animals be reunited

07:05.214 --> 07:08.701
with their owners versus us paying for them to be killed

07:09.201 --> 07:12.728
in our shelter. Um, the cats that

07:13.228 --> 07:17.071
are killed in our shelters, I— they

07:17.571 --> 07:21.264
don't deserve it. I think the root problem is people being irresponsible

07:21.345 --> 07:25.210
pet owners. Thank you. I don't think it's fair that these cats have

07:25.306 --> 07:28.991
to die simply because we are letting our unfixed

07:29.491 --> 07:33.445
pets outside. So with that being said, I hope Eagle Mountain

07:33.702 --> 07:37.227
will consider learning about how this program has helped

07:37.243 --> 07:40.976
other communities. And I think we have a great opportunity not only

07:41.024 --> 07:44.629
to save lives, but to use our tax dollars more efficiently.

07:45.719 --> 07:48.763
And I think this is a great way for Eagle Mountain to show that we

07:49.263 --> 07:52.929
are compassionate and we are. Thank you. forward-thinking as a community. Thanks.

07:53.425 --> 07:58.216
Thank you. Does council have any questions for our— Councilmember

07:58.232 --> 08:01.437
Hughes? I have a few. Councilmember Clark, can you just maybe

08:01.901 --> 08:05.747
really quickly remind me, one, is there a cost to the program

08:06.247 --> 08:09.928
for the city to participate in what you're doing? And just what does that structure

08:10.428 --> 08:13.630
look like? What's the ask of the city? I obviously, I think it is a

08:13.694 --> 08:17.331
problem. I have a couple situations in my own neighborhood, and I imagine

08:17.379 --> 08:21.146
everyone does in their neighborhoods. Um, how many do you know?

08:21.210 --> 08:24.513
How many cats are going to the— are being euthanized

08:24.738 --> 08:28.811
from our city that are going, I guess, going to that one particular shelter?

08:28.907 --> 08:32.692
And then maybe just walk us through the quickly through the process of you

08:32.836 --> 08:36.829
have a cat, it gets spayed and

08:37.102 --> 08:40.229
is just put back in place to where it was before. Is there a way

08:40.729 --> 08:43.500
that you mark those cats so, you know, if they've been spayed, so you don't

08:44.000 --> 08:47.732
have kind of— Just maybe walk us through. Uh, cost and then process.

08:48.069 --> 08:51.032
Yeah, so I can do the cost piece and then I'll let Bailey. Yeah.

08:51.593 --> 08:55.631
Um, so the cost would be free to the city. We're offering these as just

08:55.839 --> 08:58.803
good faith and, um, and free program. Um,

08:58.819 --> 09:02.392
like I said, it's a pilot program. Um, and so we have—

09:02.648 --> 09:05.852
we've made a couple proposals. The last one we put together was from American Fork.

09:05.948 --> 09:09.297
We haven't officially signed anything with them yet, so it's not currently in place.

09:10.018 --> 09:13.654
Um, but what we agreed to with them was a 1-year pilot to

09:14.154 --> 09:17.693
see how much it would decrease the workload on animal control officers,

09:18.350 --> 09:21.797
um, see if it would decrease the intake on the shelter and

09:21.893 --> 09:25.451
what percentage. And then we would reevaluate in a year and see if

09:25.499 --> 09:28.353
that program is worth their time, if they want to continue it.

09:29.010 --> 09:32.296
Um, we offered a few years ago to do this with Provo, and they wanted

09:32.796 --> 09:36.224
to do a 3-year program. Um, so it kind of just depends on what

09:36.724 --> 09:39.895
you guys are, are wanting to do. Um, but eventually,

09:40.792 --> 09:44.159
when we've done this in other areas, um, after we do it for a number

09:44.659 --> 09:48.184
of years, it actually decreases the problem enough to where we're not putting in

09:48.684 --> 09:52.177
any more resources, and the, uh, the animal control officers

09:52.225 --> 09:55.864
are able to take over the program fully without any increase

09:55.913 --> 10:00.065
to the budget because the cat population has decreased so much. So they're not having

10:00.081 --> 10:03.347
to spend as much resources. So I hope that

10:03.847 --> 10:07.175
makes sense. Yeah, and then as far as how

10:07.675 --> 10:11.339
many have entered the shelter, I know in May of this year we— it

10:11.839 --> 10:15.530
took 14 cats Um, last I checked from our

10:16.030 --> 10:18.804
grandma requests, it was 14 cats specifically from Eagle Mountain.

10:19.477 --> 10:22.991
We do offer $1 spay/neuter to the community, and we have

10:23.491 --> 10:26.746
seen an increase in those from Eagle Mountain showing

10:26.842 --> 10:30.949
up to get the community cats fixed. I don't have an exact number

10:31.110 --> 10:35.362
on how many are from Eagle Mountain, but we do have that data that

10:35.862 --> 10:38.570
we can send you. And then, as far as how we know they're fixed,

10:38.651 --> 10:42.485
when they go to the spay/neuter clinic, we ear tip their left

10:42.806 --> 10:46.831
ear, I believe. Um, and that ear tip indicates

10:47.331 --> 10:51.129
that they have been spayed/neutered, and we also vaccinate as well during

10:51.161 --> 10:54.914
that time. So, yeah, all of that would be free

10:55.414 --> 10:58.714
to the city if we do a community cat program. Um, and I believe we

10:58.762 --> 11:02.627
also have resources to pick up the animals and transport,

11:03.333 --> 11:06.396
um, as well to the spay/neuter clinic. Okay,

11:06.700 --> 11:10.324
is there any catch, anything I'm not missing? Here. I don't

11:10.824 --> 11:14.460
think so. Um, yeah, we— it would be a customizable plan.

11:14.556 --> 11:18.467
So we would love to sit down with any animal control officers or anyone

11:18.499 --> 11:22.234
involved and get an idea of what their specific issues,

11:22.331 --> 11:25.985
what their specific needs are. We are offering these services because Utah

11:26.034 --> 11:30.009
County has a really high cat population. And like Bailey said,

11:30.586 --> 11:34.337
this county is the only county in the state that is still killing healthy,

11:34.770 --> 11:38.665
treatable animals. And so we, we aren't offering these services

11:38.922 --> 11:42.291
in a lot of other counties. Most of the other counties are running similar

11:42.791 --> 11:46.420
programs through their own shelters. Um, and so

11:46.629 --> 11:49.505
there's no catch. We're just offering to help, um,

11:50.019 --> 11:53.831
decrease the community cat population. So, thank you.

11:54.059 --> 11:57.056
Yeah, thank you. Any other questions?

11:58.543 --> 12:01.762
Go ahead, Councilmember. Yeah, so can you just help me understand

12:02.262 --> 12:05.078
how you guys are funded? I mean, who's funding you? You guys are here working.

12:05.143 --> 12:08.185
It's a job. Obviously, you're collecting a paycheck. So how are you funded?

12:09.399 --> 12:12.578
As an organization, we have a number of donors.

12:12.626 --> 12:16.206
It's a national organization. We're based in Kanab, actually.

12:16.977 --> 12:20.814
So that's where our headquarters is. We actually have the largest animal sanctuary in the

12:21.314 --> 12:24.057
entire country. So if you get a chance to go down there, highly recommend it.

12:25.052 --> 12:28.568
But we have a number of donors. We also partner with a lot of rescues.

12:28.680 --> 12:31.056
And so a lot of our money goes out to the rescues.

12:31.891 --> 12:35.552
We have a ton of grant programs that we provide them, but it's mostly donors.

12:35.744 --> 12:39.218
It's community members that just want to help. Out in their own communities.

12:39.832 --> 12:42.993
Um, some people will have specific things that they want the donations to go

12:43.074 --> 12:46.849
to, like Utah-specific projects. Other people will just say, here's $20,

12:47.252 --> 12:50.499
do with it what you need. Um, so most of it is donations.

12:50.678 --> 12:52.655
We're a nonprofit, 501.

12:54.035 --> 12:57.650
Um, with other communities that have used

12:57.682 --> 13:01.458
the program or a similar program, and they transitioned

13:01.958 --> 13:05.722
from a kill shelter to a no-kill shelter. What has that process been like?

13:05.770 --> 13:09.042
And have you guys played any role in assisting those

13:09.075 --> 13:13.117
communities to know what they should be doing and how to implement that?

13:13.630 --> 13:16.870
Yeah, so we have a lot of different programs. Um, like I

13:17.370 --> 13:20.687
said, we're a nationwide organization, um,

13:20.784 --> 13:23.687
and we've played a hand in a lot of shelters, um,

13:23.911 --> 13:27.857
going from no-kill to— or going from not yet no-kill to

13:28.146 --> 13:31.899
no-kill. Um, and for those not aware of that term, um, no-kill is

13:32.092 --> 13:36.207
just language that we use in shelter world. Um, and it means that shelters

13:36.707 --> 13:39.632
are hitting a 90% save rate. So 90% of the animals that are going in

13:40.132 --> 13:43.617
there are having live positive outcomes. Um, we recognize that

13:44.117 --> 13:47.428
some animals are going to be sick and they need to be euthanized and they're

13:47.928 --> 13:51.302
suffering, or there are unfortunately dangerous animals that we don't want back into

13:51.350 --> 13:55.303
the, to the community. So that's what that 10% buffer kind of accounts

13:55.803 --> 13:59.103
for. Um, and so we just recently, we helped

13:59.603 --> 14:01.957
2 states. Montana recently just became an entire no-kill state. Wow.

14:03.019 --> 14:04.310
And I think Idaho as well.

14:06.165 --> 14:09.731
And we've played a role in a number of Utah shelters. I think when

14:10.231 --> 14:13.808
we started in Utah, about a 3rd of the shelters were no-kill, and now

14:13.840 --> 14:17.092
we're up to all but 2, and there's 59 shelters throughout

14:17.592 --> 14:21.423
the state. And how that process works is we work with

14:21.520 --> 14:25.862
each individual shelter to assess their needs. So sometimes they need veterinary

14:26.137 --> 14:30.143
assistance. And so we have an embed program. We will send veterinary

14:30.753 --> 14:34.396
assistants or actual veterinarians into a shelter to provide training,

14:35.247 --> 14:38.875
to help kind of with that workload and then

14:39.068 --> 14:43.225
ease them off of it and get them going. We also provide grant programs.

14:43.273 --> 14:46.917
So if it's an issue of funding, which I know a lot of municipal shelters

14:47.013 --> 14:50.577
have not enough funding, and so we provide grant programs.

14:50.657 --> 14:54.028
So just things like that based off of what individual shelters need.

14:54.719 --> 14:58.154
Awesome. Thank you. Any other questions before we Is

14:58.170 --> 15:01.317
there any direction or thoughts that council would like to convey

15:01.414 --> 15:04.978
to staff? I'm supportive of this, but so would we bring this

15:05.090 --> 15:08.382
back up at the legislative portion of our scheduled or

15:08.882 --> 15:12.862
our policy meeting? I think we can make a motion then. Yep. Because I'm supportive.

15:14.098 --> 15:17.631
Same. Awesome. Okay, thank you. Thank you so much.

15:17.839 --> 15:21.211
We'll invite Kiara Poole and

15:21.243 --> 15:24.873
Elizabeth McNanny, just a clarification.

15:25.002 --> 15:28.260
The last one, you mean at a future meeting? Because it's not on the agenda.

15:28.340 --> 15:31.808
Legislative priorities. Oh, legislative priorities. Okay.

15:34.128 --> 15:36.446
Yep. All right, as they're making their way up, they're going to talk to us

15:36.575 --> 15:40.164
about our program year 2026 Community Development Block

15:40.664 --> 15:44.256
Grant funding priorities and project planning. So, ladies.

15:45.514 --> 15:48.448
Sweet. Thank you, guys. Um,

15:50.552 --> 15:53.903
Okay, we thought this might happen, so we have a quick little

15:53.936 --> 15:57.834
backup detour. I'm gonna let Elizabeth introduce—

15:57.931 --> 16:01.169
she's gonna be presenting, and then I will hook this up. Okay,

16:02.691 --> 16:05.857
I'm Elizabeth McNanny. I work with Resource Consultants,

16:06.001 --> 16:09.586
and I've been working with the City of Eagle Mountain for

16:09.876 --> 16:14.031
the last few months and kind of transitioning,

16:14.128 --> 16:17.902
helping to transition the CDBG program back in-house.

16:18.788 --> 16:24.091
My background is I've worked with HUD programs for probably

16:24.123 --> 16:27.760
15 years. I've worked with federal programs kind

16:28.260 --> 16:31.911
of all across the board, everything from Federal Highways to USDA,

16:32.767 --> 16:36.207
as well as HUD and Treasury for about 20 years.

16:37.223 --> 16:40.525
And I am excited to be here. Thank you,

16:40.589 --> 16:42.845
Council, for having me here today.

16:51.518 --> 16:54.690
Yay! Okay, we can just go right past the front

16:55.190 --> 16:57.409
page. Do I, do I have a clicker? Does this work?

16:58.954 --> 17:02.578
Okay. All right, we'll talk

17:03.078 --> 17:06.767
about just the overview of entitlement funds. It'll be a quick

17:06.912 --> 17:10.588
overview and an overview of what's in the annual action

17:10.604 --> 17:14.347
plan. Kind of next steps, because you'll be seeing me again in

17:14.444 --> 17:18.131
just a couple of weeks, and I'd welcome any

17:18.243 --> 17:18.968
feedback.

17:22.070 --> 17:26.140
So Eagle Mountain is an entitlement community, and to be an entitlement

17:26.640 --> 17:30.420
community, you must have 50,000 people as a

17:30.550 --> 17:34.018
city and not also

17:34.050 --> 17:36.679
be involved with an urban county.

17:37.454 --> 17:41.571
So there are cities that have the access to

17:41.620 --> 17:45.218
entitlement funds, but they choose to still run

17:45.718 --> 17:49.732
their funds under an urban county. So Eagle Mountain has been an entitlement

17:50.232 --> 17:53.610
city for 2 years. We have just started the 3rd

17:53.674 --> 17:56.377
year, and we'll talk about that as we go through this.

17:58.610 --> 18:01.154
So CDBG is a formula process,

18:01.846 --> 18:05.856
and there is a YouTube video about how HUD determines

18:05.969 --> 18:09.867
the annual allocation. It is Super riveting, if anyone

18:10.367 --> 18:14.485
is interested. So this really targets—

18:14.533 --> 18:18.161
these funds target housing and community development

18:18.661 --> 18:22.064
activities, really aimed at households that earn

18:22.242 --> 18:25.047
80% or below of the area median income.

18:26.083 --> 18:30.198
And HUD funds are received directly rather

18:30.360 --> 18:33.017
than through the state as an entitlement community.

18:35.875 --> 18:39.466
So, just to kind of give you a baseline for what the 80%

18:40.049 --> 18:43.599
means, you can see here, these are the updated numbers.

18:43.712 --> 18:47.760
They came out last month. A single-member household

18:48.260 --> 18:52.344
can earn up to $66,750 and

18:52.361 --> 18:55.734
qualify as 80% area median income.

18:56.512 --> 19:00.631
So, anything below that is eligible for Any

19:00.727 --> 19:04.482
household below that is eligible to receive funding

19:04.691 --> 19:07.334
through CDBG with certain caveats.

19:10.438 --> 19:13.925
So, the CDBG program, the primary objectives are

19:14.071 --> 19:16.959
to develop viable urban communities,

19:17.721 --> 19:21.737
primarily for low and moderate income persons through decent

19:21.754 --> 19:25.540
housing and a suitable living environment and expanded

19:25.637 --> 19:29.429
economic opportunities. A lot of times that's talking about

19:29.996 --> 19:33.361
Housing, public services, and the built environment.

19:34.483 --> 19:39.451
There are national objectives. Every activity that is undertaken must

19:39.951 --> 19:43.794
meet a national objective. The most common one is benefit

19:44.294 --> 19:48.377
to LMI households. The next one, prevent and eliminate slum

19:48.986 --> 19:52.415
or blight, is very restricted with

19:52.511 --> 19:55.997
very specific caveats that must, you know, things that must

19:56.062 --> 19:59.269
be met before that can be used. And the 3rd one is

19:59.317 --> 20:02.783
address an urgent need. You would never use that one unless

20:03.283 --> 20:06.955
HUD tells you you can. It's typically a natural

20:07.004 --> 20:07.637
disaster.

20:10.440 --> 20:14.505
So the core principles are again to benefit LMI

20:15.005 --> 20:19.152
persons and communities and community participation and

20:19.748 --> 20:23.151
leveraging and partners. You'll see in the next couple of slides,

20:23.594 --> 20:27.554
there's not enough money in the CDBG program for Eagle Mountain city

20:28.054 --> 20:30.970
to solve any one challenge that you might face,

20:31.501 --> 20:34.224
but it's about strategic and targeted investment.

20:37.590 --> 20:41.301
Things that are eligible: housing rehabilitation,

20:42.780 --> 20:46.218
economic development, public services.

20:46.395 --> 20:50.694
Here's kind of a list of public services. This is definitely not exhaustive.

20:51.080 --> 20:54.095
There's a much longer list. But these are some.

20:54.945 --> 20:59.720
You have supported subsistence payments, meaning utility

20:59.752 --> 21:03.486
assistance, as well as food pantry services

21:04.078 --> 21:07.411
within Eagle Mountain City through CDBG previously.

21:08.149 --> 21:12.299
Public facilities and neighborhood improvements. You have also worked with

21:12.892 --> 21:16.529
the senior center with upgrades there.

21:17.459 --> 21:18.853
Things that are ineligible.

21:21.368 --> 21:23.915
Fundraising. Normal expenses of the government.

21:24.305 --> 21:27.566
Income payments. We can't write checks to anyone.

21:29.070 --> 21:33.210
Buildings or portions thereof used for the general conduct of government.

21:34.116 --> 21:37.998
Typically, the purchase of equipment is disallowed.

21:39.370 --> 21:42.682
Maintenance and repairs. So the difference is, if you

21:42.698 --> 21:46.060
have a park in an eligible area and the service

21:46.173 --> 21:49.544
area is eligible, You could do upgrades, but you can't

21:49.560 --> 21:53.079
mow the lawn. So that's kind of the difference there.

21:56.920 --> 22:00.637
Activities affecting multifamily structures or public facilities,

22:01.218 --> 22:05.162
including infrastructure, must guarantee that

22:05.194 --> 22:08.310
51% of the beneficiaries

22:08.423 --> 22:12.248
are low to moderate income, and activities related

22:12.297 --> 22:16.454
to single-family housing developments must serve 100%

22:17.527 --> 22:20.850
low to moderate income, because it's looked at household

22:20.995 --> 22:24.849
by household. And many jurisdictions require that

22:25.349 --> 22:28.365
their public services primarily try to serve at least

22:28.865 --> 22:32.315
100% or close to 100% LMI

22:32.815 --> 22:36.650
clients. So food pantries and, you know, the utility payments

22:37.150 --> 22:40.359
and things like that. And no project is really eligible if

22:40.423 --> 22:43.935
it doesn't meet that 51% income benefit.

22:46.803 --> 22:49.768
So for 2026, program year 2026,

22:50.560 --> 22:53.923
which started July 1st, the CDBG

22:54.423 --> 22:55.458
award is $246,979.

22:58.741 --> 23:02.281
Of that, planning and administration can only use

23:02.895 --> 23:07.214
up to 20%. There's also a cap on public services.

23:07.879 --> 23:11.866
So public services are capped at 15% of your entitlement

23:12.366 --> 23:15.709
award. So that seems— it's low. A lot

23:16.209 --> 23:20.317
of communities would like to use more on direct services, but HUD

23:20.493 --> 23:24.700
to this date has not made that possible. There are some

23:24.748 --> 23:28.521
changes hopefully coming down the line that

23:29.021 --> 23:32.648
may change that in the future. You right

23:32.696 --> 23:37.256
now have an opportunity over the next year because

23:37.272 --> 23:41.029
you have a significant amount of prior year resources.

23:41.767 --> 23:45.904
You have a bucket of funding that's about $220,000

23:46.513 --> 23:50.041
that has not been allocated and has not been spent.

23:50.683 --> 23:54.018
So that increases the amount you have available

23:54.756 --> 23:58.668
over the next program year. Prior year resources

23:59.262 --> 24:02.870
unfortunately do not increase your public service cap, and they

24:03.370 --> 24:06.750
do not increase your administrative cap. So your public service

24:06.783 --> 24:10.530
funding available For program year 2026

24:11.030 --> 24:14.455
is about $36,000, and administration and

24:14.488 --> 24:18.058
planning is about $49,000. So you can see

24:18.558 --> 24:23.009
there you have a little over $380,000 available for other

24:23.509 --> 24:27.486
activities. And in

24:27.986 --> 24:31.808
a 5-year consolidated plan, basically you have the

24:31.888 --> 24:35.454
overall plan, the 5-year plan, and then every year

24:35.583 --> 24:38.027
you have to have an action plan that drills down a little bit deeper.

24:39.795 --> 24:43.507
For HUD to tell them your priority projects for

24:44.007 --> 24:47.720
that year. So we are in year 3, and you will see me in

24:48.220 --> 24:52.313
a couple of weeks with a proposal for the 3rd-year annual

24:52.813 --> 24:55.961
action plan. Here are some

24:55.994 --> 24:59.911
of the components. You've got, of course, your executive summary and

25:00.008 --> 25:03.423
lead organization, expected resources. You'll see

25:03.536 --> 25:06.243
exactly what we just went through on the previous slide.

25:06.360 --> 25:10.274
Slide about resources, annual goals and objectives,

25:10.499 --> 25:13.850
and projects. You'll also— in the plan,

25:13.963 --> 25:17.454
there's some sections for housing and non-housing

25:17.582 --> 25:21.778
needs. This is not like a housing needs assessment. This is very

25:22.003 --> 25:24.721
prescriptive. It's HUD speak.

25:28.557 --> 25:31.950
So you start with needs which are only in the consolidated

25:31.982 --> 25:35.511
plan. And then you have goals in your consolidated

25:36.011 --> 25:39.583
plan that flow down to each of your annual action plans.

25:40.112 --> 25:43.639
So you could have 4 or 5 goals, and only 3

25:44.489 --> 25:48.064
might be captured in any individual action plan. They don't

25:48.192 --> 25:51.639
all have to flow down. And then you have projects.

25:51.799 --> 25:55.438
Those are— these are the buckets. You have projects in your

25:55.727 --> 25:57.939
annual action plan, housing.

25:58.535 --> 26:02.079
Public infrastructure, public services, things like

26:02.579 --> 26:06.141
that. And then the activities are really a function on the other

26:06.641 --> 26:10.651
side. It is the reporting side of ITIS, the discrete

26:11.151 --> 26:12.331
activities that you're undertaking.

26:15.777 --> 26:19.602
This is kind of a flow way to look at this. Create a suitable

26:20.102 --> 26:23.693
living environment might be a need. Public facilities and public

26:23.775 --> 26:26.160
infrastructure improvements may be a goal.

26:27.248 --> 26:31.138
Public infrastructure improvements to benefit LMI

26:31.508 --> 26:34.578
Eagle Mountain City residents could be a project,

26:35.204 --> 26:38.786
and ADA upgrades in X area

26:39.335 --> 26:42.368
of Eagle Mountain City could actually be the activity.

26:43.650 --> 26:46.800
And ADA upgrades you can do pretty much

26:46.993 --> 26:50.434
anywhere, as long as they're not specifically

26:50.466 --> 26:54.227
targeted to kids. Because kids

26:54.356 --> 26:57.368
