up against the 15%. And so far, and this year you

38:16.330 --> 38:20.336
did have an application process. This was before I was working with

38:20.464 --> 38:24.422
Eagle Mountain City, and you received 3

38:24.470 --> 38:27.851
applications. I don't know if you guys recall, they came and presented.

38:28.796 --> 38:32.369
Yeah, so those, and those are the— and they're looking

38:32.417 --> 38:35.205
to be able to obviously utilize public service dollars. So, right.

38:36.746 --> 38:39.733
Awesome. Thank you. Any other questions before we have them sit down?

38:40.584 --> 38:43.940
Thank you. Thank you guys. All right, we'll invite Beth Fuchs up. She's going

38:43.972 --> 38:46.750
to talk to us about the wildland urban interface standards.

38:47.649 --> 38:51.791
And as you guys remember, this is bringing this back to kind of address small

38:51.920 --> 38:53.799
lots, and she's seeking some feedback from us.

39:42.828 --> 39:45.648
Apologize for the delay, but we got it worked out. Okay,

39:45.793 --> 39:49.869
so we have discussed this before when we were doing our

39:49.901 --> 39:53.389
WUI map. We Brett and I spoke with

39:53.889 --> 39:57.706
the fire officials and talked to them about ways we could make our city safer

39:58.660 --> 40:01.113
for fire hazards, particularly wildfires.

40:02.494 --> 40:05.708
And we came— I came and spoke with the council

40:06.208 --> 40:09.821
before, and there were some concerns with some of

40:09.903 --> 40:13.642
the things that were presented. And so I've gone back to the dry board,

40:13.690 --> 40:16.798
have a few other suggestions to see if

40:16.862 --> 40:20.192
it's something we want to do, or if we'd Don't want to go

40:20.208 --> 40:23.427
in that direction. Sorry, it's not advancing.

40:45.958 --> 40:49.650
There we go. Okay, so that was the background, and I did also

40:50.150 --> 40:53.696
want to let you know that HB 215 that was passed by

40:53.904 --> 40:57.324
the state does have some changes to standards

40:57.340 --> 41:01.899
that we're not allowed to prohibit residents

41:02.399 --> 41:05.832
from clearing vegetation if they're within the WUI boundaries.

41:05.944 --> 41:09.155
So I will be bringing that back to the council, but I wanted to see

41:09.655 --> 41:11.547
if we were going to do anything else to see if we bring them back

41:12.047 --> 41:15.141
together, or if I'll just bring that one back. So, after our discussion,

41:15.317 --> 41:19.344
I got thinking and want— I'm a visual person. I wanted

41:19.844 --> 41:22.633
to see what this would look like, what the impact would be on the residents.

41:22.777 --> 41:26.531
And I know one of the concerns was having standards that would limit

41:26.820 --> 41:31.264
certain residents from being able to fully utilize their land,

41:31.938 --> 41:34.633
where if they live somewhere else, they would be able to do different things.

41:35.452 --> 41:39.222
And so, as I looked at that, I looked at some

41:39.722 --> 41:44.881
of our larger lots, Particularly those that already had a 35-foot

41:45.154 --> 41:49.193
rear setback, because that would then allow

41:49.337 --> 41:52.880
them to have 30 feet of defensible space for their property.

41:53.649 --> 41:57.560
And so, as I got looking at that, another thing that comes

41:57.592 --> 42:01.263
into play with that is accessory dwelling units,

42:02.000 --> 42:05.318
because our current code requires that those meet

42:05.430 --> 42:09.022
the setback standards for the primary dwelling unit. So,

42:09.360 --> 42:13.006
in that, that would prohibit from putting an ADU

42:13.071 --> 42:15.529
in an extremely hazardous area where,

42:16.766 --> 42:20.429
where they would be exposed to a fire, and that's already in code.

42:20.927 --> 42:24.461
And so I took kind of a standard lot with the minimum frontage for a

42:24.961 --> 42:28.494
half-acre property. And then one, some of these areas are more

42:28.994 --> 42:31.964
challenging areas, so you could have some oddly shaped lots. So I kind of did

42:32.012 --> 42:35.225
the 2 extremes to put them together. And if you look,

42:35.386 --> 42:38.837
The orange areas are where someone would be able to place an

42:39.337 --> 42:42.833
accessory dwelling unit or any

42:43.266 --> 42:47.038
accessory structure and have it 30 feet

42:47.423 --> 42:51.420
away from that and be able to have that 30 feet of defensible space.

42:52.094 --> 42:56.010
We also talked about, though, that really where it's 30 feet of defensible

42:56.026 --> 42:59.236
space, not necessarily a 30-foot setback that we're concerned with.

43:00.023 --> 43:03.568
I also looked at The opposite. So what if instead it

43:04.068 --> 43:07.963
was 30 feet spacing between structures? And so

43:07.995 --> 43:10.882
these are— that's what this would look like.

43:11.588 --> 43:14.844
And as I was looking at this, I realized

43:14.956 --> 43:18.549
most people aren't going to build a huge accessory structure in the middle

43:19.049 --> 43:22.576
of their backyard. You're going to probably put it closer to the home or closer

43:23.076 --> 43:26.650
to the rear of the lot. And so on a larger lot, I think where

43:27.150 --> 43:30.451
people would naturally place those structures. would allow them to have 30

43:30.951 --> 43:34.878
feet of defensible space. So we could, if you wanted,

43:34.910 --> 43:37.468
instead of looking at a setback standard,

43:38.227 --> 43:41.973
look at a minimum lot standard. And if

43:42.183 --> 43:45.782
they had the— we would be looking instead of mandating something,

43:45.959 --> 43:49.937
facilitating it, make sure that lots in those areas were large enough

43:49.985 --> 43:53.576
that they would be able to easily enjoy

43:53.786 --> 43:57.242
the use of their property and still be able to provide 30 feet of defensible

43:57.258 --> 44:01.277
space, or the other. Recommendations by the state when

44:01.777 --> 44:06.303
they come in. And if we were to move forward

44:06.803 --> 44:09.131
with this, that would kind of take care of rear lots. We would need to

44:09.631 --> 44:13.147
decide if there could be whole developments that wouldn't have a side yard

44:13.647 --> 44:16.655
that would be impacted. But if it did, how would we want to address protecting

44:16.688 --> 44:20.118
those? We wouldn't necessarily need to accommodate it all on the property.

44:20.537 --> 44:24.326
We could do it with like a trail or a road or something so

44:24.826 --> 44:29.267
that there is still 30 feet of space between the home and

44:29.445 --> 44:33.174
the improved wildlands. And I

44:33.352 --> 44:37.160
also wanted to just clarify, I'm just— I

44:37.660 --> 44:41.711
was talking about having this only apply to boundary lots, and that

44:41.824 --> 44:45.276
what I brought before only applied to those adjacent to

44:45.293 --> 44:49.003
wildlands, not property that's against

44:49.165 --> 44:53.083
unbuilt areas. However, the state will be coming in and assessing

44:53.201 --> 44:56.649
and charging These were all properties

44:56.746 --> 45:00.079
within the WUI. So we, if we did something, we need to

45:00.579 --> 45:03.798
decide, do we just want it to be there? Would we want it to just

45:03.831 --> 45:07.591
be along wherever there isn't

45:07.624 --> 45:11.610
development? Because if it's against an area that isn't

45:12.110 --> 45:14.864
developed, not could be developed, but we don't know when,

45:16.117 --> 45:19.486
you have that kind of interim hazard. And for example,

45:19.503 --> 45:22.794
what happened in Roy yesterday, even if it's not a wildland,

45:22.843 --> 45:26.595
you can have fast-moving fires come in. So we need

45:27.095 --> 45:28.728
to think about and consider things like that.

45:32.032 --> 45:35.712
And it— Councilmember Wood brought up and recommended

45:36.212 --> 45:40.419
last time that we had these discussions of informing residents

45:41.014 --> 45:44.886
that they're in the WUI, that them know. And I think

45:45.386 --> 45:49.561
that's an excellent idea because they may choose to place an accessory structure

45:49.625 --> 45:53.193
in a different location with that knowledge, or choose a different material type. or things

45:53.225 --> 45:56.989
like that, and they'll understand and can maybe when they're

45:57.489 --> 46:01.828
putting in their landscaping, take into account some of those state recommendations

46:02.580 --> 46:04.299
instead of having to change things later.

46:06.626 --> 46:10.136
And so I just also put in the WUI map to remind

46:10.636 --> 46:14.099
us where these areas are. And again, some of these areas could fall out of

46:14.147 --> 46:17.584
the WUI, especially as they're being developed, but they're probably going to be

46:18.084 --> 46:21.392
certain properties that are always going to have that wildfire lit. risk,

46:21.441 --> 46:24.642
especially if they're near washes or hillsides, things like that.

46:26.307 --> 46:29.346
And these I just put in for reference if we needed them.

46:30.220 --> 46:33.679
And so those were kind of my thoughts based on our previous discussion.

46:34.844 --> 46:37.964
How does the council want to proceed? Do we just want to move with state

46:37.996 --> 46:41.012
requirements, or do you want to consider any additional standards?

46:42.537 --> 46:46.834
Can I just ask a question on the unplanned buildable

46:46.882 --> 46:50.486
area? Is that a requirement of the state? Or is the requirement

46:50.986 --> 46:55.046
just up against unbuildable wildlands? So it's just where we've defined

46:55.546 --> 47:00.023
it on our map. That was just to kind of illustrate different

47:00.523 --> 47:03.588
situations we could run into, because some of those WUI areas are going to be

47:04.088 --> 47:07.955
surrounded by developed land. Some will have undeveloped lands,

47:08.148 --> 47:11.921
but they— that aren't in the WUI. So it's

47:12.421 --> 47:13.206
just different things to consider.

47:16.212 --> 47:19.800
I'm wondering if it would make sense to whoever owns that land to have

47:20.300 --> 47:23.703
a 30-foot defensible space instead

47:24.203 --> 47:27.760
of putting that on the property owners. Oh, like, so there'd be

47:28.260 --> 47:32.035
a buffer built into the development around the edges? Yeah, that'd be my preference.

47:32.437 --> 47:35.780
Could we give like open space credit or something like that

47:36.280 --> 47:39.764
with that? What do you do when it's a government agency or it's

47:40.264 --> 47:40.635
a different municipality?

47:44.188 --> 47:47.349
Because a lot of times you're talking about the area around

47:47.849 --> 47:51.412
the outside, like Silver Lake, Saratoga. You could have that right up against

47:51.460 --> 47:55.520
BLM land. You could have that against county land. So there's

47:56.020 --> 47:59.643
some challenges. I agree. I think the responsibility needs to be shared

47:59.997 --> 48:03.350
both with those who have structures on their land and those that don't.

48:05.570 --> 48:08.048
Yeah. Any other thoughts from council? Councilmember Wright?

48:11.200 --> 48:14.665
Councilmember Whiting. So what is— so what if you have 2 WUI lots back

48:15.165 --> 48:18.018
up against each other? Seems like you could have 15 feet on one side and

48:18.518 --> 48:22.670
15 feet on another side of defensible space. And would that— would

48:23.170 --> 48:26.360
that work? You have 2 WUI lots backed up. I mean, how would

48:26.860 --> 48:29.617
you get 2 WUI lots backed up? That wouldn't work. That wouldn't be a need.

48:29.665 --> 48:32.986
I'm just curious, how would that become the requirement? Because it's really the border,

48:33.242 --> 48:36.226
right? It's the outside. So it's the WUI area, but I guess as you develop

48:36.726 --> 48:40.011
it, that WUI area sort of decreases. But your buildings are

48:40.139 --> 48:43.249
permanent. But your buildings

48:43.281 --> 48:47.048
are there. Like, if you are on that boundary

48:47.548 --> 48:51.457
lot, you put in your 30-foot setback, that building's there.

48:52.819 --> 48:56.170
I guess that's a risk you take buying that

48:56.670 --> 48:59.809
property. Beth, I'm— I mean, I want to share a couple thoughts.

49:00.097 --> 49:03.448
I'm a little confused. I'm going to— and that's easy

49:03.544 --> 49:07.419
to do with me. Um, it seems to me that the half-acre

49:07.919 --> 49:12.007
standards that we already have create a 35-foot backyard

49:12.507 --> 49:16.258
setback. So I don't see that as an issue. The issue is really the side

49:16.758 --> 49:21.474
yard, is what I'm hearing you say, and then perhaps whether

49:21.974 --> 49:25.389
or not we require half-acre lots or larger on the periphery.

49:25.679 --> 49:28.969
I think that's really the question that we are looking at.

49:31.516 --> 49:34.517
I feel a little ambiguous about this because it's, do we,

49:34.582 --> 49:38.857
do we, do we impose additional standards on something that we're

49:39.357 --> 49:43.015
not required to for safety? That's the first question that perhaps we need

49:43.515 --> 49:45.692
to address as a council and, and speak to.

49:47.149 --> 49:51.236
The other issue too is that we know it's going to change. I mean,

49:51.430 --> 49:54.967
the reality of it is you impose something on somebody that is a very temporary

49:55.064 --> 49:59.449
nature. That, that's the concern I have, is that it

49:59.949 --> 50:00.528
Because it's just what Councilmember Whiting said. Well, what happens when you get 2 back

50:01.028 --> 50:04.750
to back, right? What happens when we annex and it

50:04.766 --> 50:08.282
changes? Um, so I just— it's a moving target.

50:09.567 --> 50:13.275
Uh, that's why I, I think it— I understand the purpose

50:13.775 --> 50:17.096
of it. We're trying to mitigate fire spreading, and,

50:17.209 --> 50:20.901
and I think everybody's on board with that. I think we've demonstrated

50:21.401 --> 50:23.855
that, but we've got to be careful with legislation that has a moving target.

50:24.590 --> 50:28.832
On it that can change, and we're putting a

50:28.896 --> 50:32.426
burden on a homeowner to do something that is a temporary thing

50:32.847 --> 50:35.464
that they are going to have the— I mean, are they going to be reimbursed

50:35.609 --> 50:38.756
when it gets built up against them? The answer is no, right?

50:38.852 --> 50:43.000
So they're going to have to make this investment into that until the

50:43.500 --> 50:46.785
developer behind them decides to complete theirs. And once that's done, they're no longer in

50:46.882 --> 50:50.522
WUI. So I just— things like that, I have some

50:51.022 --> 50:54.529
consternation about because it's I don't,

50:55.186 --> 50:58.727
I don't love imposing restrictions that cost money that

50:59.227 --> 51:01.355
are temporary. We know it's going to eventually change.

51:02.476 --> 51:06.210
But I also think there needs to be something in place that at very minimum,

51:06.466 --> 51:10.055
they should be required to clear it, but they should be able to build something

51:10.472 --> 51:14.221
on their land also. Like, I don't know, I just, I'm struggling

51:14.253 --> 51:17.378
with this one. Beth, you had something? Yeah, I just wanted

51:17.878 --> 51:20.969
to clarify. That's why I was looked more at

51:21.469 --> 51:25.671
the lot size, because there wouldn't be any additional requirements

51:26.105 --> 51:29.957
on those residents than any other resident who owned

51:30.457 --> 51:33.889
a half-acre lot. The, the 35-foot setback's

51:33.969 --> 51:37.837
already in place. It just allows them to be able to

51:38.142 --> 51:41.095
put in, to clear the land if they wanted to.

51:41.834 --> 51:45.332
And then also the side, if we had it through a road or a trail,

51:45.702 --> 51:49.938
then it's the developer who's putting that in. They would have the normal 15-foot

51:50.035 --> 51:54.174
garage side setback as other residents. So that

51:54.206 --> 51:57.303
was more the direction. So it shouldn't change anything.

51:58.281 --> 52:01.522
They wanted a half-acre lot, they'd buy a half-acre lot and have the same rights

52:02.022 --> 52:05.758
as anybody else in the city who had that. Could we consider— I

52:06.258 --> 52:09.287
mean, my thought process is, is could we just consider as part of a development

52:09.787 --> 52:13.459
application that the developer make an assessment about the WUI and that

52:13.959 --> 52:17.786
we require that to be reviewed as part of any new development that comes forward.

52:18.304 --> 52:21.410
Because I think at that point in time, you can look at each individual piece

52:21.685 --> 52:25.332
on its merits, and understanding

52:25.380 --> 52:28.417
that it, it may change, it may not change. I don't know,

52:28.514 --> 52:30.387
I'm just throwing out— I'm spitballing here.

52:32.863 --> 52:36.327
Regardless of what we do, I think staff should be sure to inform the

52:36.827 --> 52:40.001
council that we're dealing with a WUI area. Now, some of these areas already have

52:40.033 --> 52:43.704
vestings and entitlements, Some do not, but I think

52:44.204 --> 52:47.749
it would be worth working with the developers of those properties

52:47.861 --> 52:51.343
to try to make them safe. And I'm not saying

52:51.456 --> 52:55.083
these are the best and the only ways. I just think it's something that we

52:55.583 --> 52:59.079
have an opportunity most other cities don't to take these into

52:59.579 --> 53:02.914
consideration as we're approving development. And so,

53:03.331 --> 53:06.958
just being conscientious of that and trying to find ways to make sure that

53:07.458 --> 53:10.714
our residents are safe. Thank you. Councilmember,

53:10.875 --> 53:13.813
I mean, I do like the idea of spacing the buildings apart,

53:14.824 --> 53:18.099
having that 30-foot setback between the primary residence and

53:18.179 --> 53:21.535
a barn, or— to me, that makes sense. I have

53:21.872 --> 53:25.564
no issues with that. I can't remember who

53:26.064 --> 53:29.738
brought it up, but somebody said something about requiring half-acre lots on the periphery.

53:29.834 --> 53:33.286
Is that something that we want to pursue and have be a

53:33.786 --> 53:34.682
part of this conversation, or?

53:37.476 --> 53:40.526
I'm almost thinking we have

53:41.026 --> 53:44.138
so few. I mean, we have— there's areas, right? But we almost need to look

53:44.638 --> 53:47.189
at this as each development application comes through. Okay.

53:48.056 --> 53:51.588
That's what I'm thinking. All right, anybody else have any thoughts

53:52.088 --> 53:54.413
they want to share? Well, I mean, I mean, if you just take an example

53:54.913 --> 53:57.576
of the Horizon development, right, it has entitlements on it. I mean, some of those

53:57.608 --> 54:00.578
lots are going to be bigger. There is some open space that is at least

54:01.078 --> 54:04.478
being considered there. And obviously there's changes that are occurring there,

54:04.607 --> 54:07.915
right? There's at least contemplation, but that's kind of

54:08.415 --> 54:11.480
an island in the city. And at what point does that no longer become an

54:11.528 --> 54:15.089
island? And do we really impose those standards on an island? Those are

54:15.589 --> 54:19.116
the questions that are going through my mind, and I don't know

54:19.197 --> 54:20.536
that one size fits all.

54:23.064 --> 54:26.419
And maybe the contemplation

54:26.919 --> 54:30.573
should be against those Those lands that are BLM or others.

54:30.621 --> 54:34.280
I mean, maybe that's where the contemplation should start. I don't know. So, well,

54:35.131 --> 54:39.031
so what I'm hearing is that it's

54:39.127 --> 54:42.946
a unique situation every single time. So we really probably should

54:42.979 --> 54:46.943
be adding a review of this to every development process

54:47.456 --> 54:50.842
and ask staff to alert us so that we can have the

54:51.342 --> 54:54.932
discussion when we have the factors unique to that area. Is there anything

54:55.110 --> 54:56.698
else we want to instruct staff to work on?

55:00.582 --> 55:05.375
I liked Councilmember Wood's suggestion about a plat notation and disclosure

55:05.875 --> 55:09.019
requirements. So, you know, buyers know that they're in a WUI area.

55:11.917 --> 55:14.957
All right, it sounds like— did you get the feedback you needed from us?

55:15.900 --> 55:19.038
Yes, so I'll bring back the state requirements and the plat notation.

55:20.780 --> 55:24.878
Okay. And if there's a way for us to make it a required step in

55:25.378 --> 55:28.638
our design review process or whatever, because we still want

55:29.138 --> 55:31.712
to know if it's an issue and we want to make sure it's part of

55:32.149 --> 55:36.008
what we're doing. So I'm gonna ask a question of David. Do you mind?

55:36.526 --> 55:38.375
Do you mind if he comes up so I can ask him a question?

55:38.790 --> 55:40.320
Oh, David Yuleberry. Absolutely. Go ahead.

55:41.077 --> 55:44.832
Nice shoes,

55:44.864 --> 55:48.385
David. Love your shoes. Yeah, just in

55:48.885 --> 55:51.878
thinking about this, I mean, I understand all of the reasons that we're trying to

55:51.910 --> 55:55.436
mitigate some of these things, but it feels like we're putting a huge

55:55.936 --> 55:59.346
amount of, uh, onus on small lot

55:59.362 --> 56:02.727
property owners, which generally speaking are not

56:03.227 --> 56:06.701
the ones that are spreading the fire. It's usually on large lots. Have you

56:06.717 --> 56:10.707
heard anything from the state, from any of the fire organizations

56:10.723 --> 56:14.325
where they're talking about Creating defensible spaces on

56:14.825 --> 56:18.598
some of these large pieces of land, whether they belong to the— an

56:18.711 --> 56:21.699
individual property owner or to the state or to the federal government.

56:22.261 --> 56:25.375
Are you aware of anything where they're talking about that? Or— If they're in

56:25.407 --> 56:29.505
the WUI area, they're required for those, whether whatever the size of the lot

56:30.005 --> 56:33.407
may be. So those larger lots are required for those 30 feet if they're in

56:33.907 --> 56:37.120
a designated WUI area. It just depends on what city

56:37.217 --> 56:40.649
they're in. And they would designate those areas.

56:40.857 --> 56:43.233
Okay, that's good to know, because I don't think that was clear to us.

56:43.570 --> 56:47.567
How are we notifying them? Because basically, when we started with the map, you get

56:48.145 --> 56:51.580
an area that they would define by the definition in

56:52.080 --> 56:55.833
a WUI area. But there were plenty of areas in our city map

56:56.333 --> 57:00.376
that were— it wasn't actually— the topography wasn't dangerous.

57:00.601 --> 57:04.485
It was prior to development that they were marked as

57:04.501 --> 57:08.610
that. So there was a few areas in our own map that we changed out

57:08.675 --> 57:12.189
of that. From— that was initially red

57:12.221 --> 57:16.121
flagged as in the WUI area, and that's up to the individual cities

57:16.153 --> 57:19.090
to go through those maps and make those changes.

57:19.170 --> 57:22.894
So it's not just specific to homeowners, it's to actual just

57:23.394 --> 57:26.184
landowners. Yeah. Okay. And so we can— and you go through and you have to

57:26.684 --> 57:30.469
make your argument, obviously, as to why it should be exempt from there.

57:31.062 --> 57:34.673
And we went through that in quite a few areas. Thanks for

57:35.173 --> 57:38.518
the clarity. Yeah. Anything else? All right,

57:38.840 --> 57:42.430
we're moving on to our next item. Uh, Rob Hobbs will

57:42.462 --> 57:46.539
be presenting a discussion item, proposed private purchase

57:46.652 --> 57:49.795
of 0.13 acres of the ranch's golf course.

57:52.511 --> 57:53.373
Mr. Hobbs.

58:01.671 --> 58:04.928
Thank you, Mayor Pro Tem, Council. Staff was approached by,

58:05.787 --> 58:10.672
uh, Ms. Bree Folsom, owner of property at 3544 East

58:10.720 --> 58:14.527
Royal Dornoch Drive, with the request

58:15.027 --> 58:19.573
to purchase adjoining or abutting land, some 1.13

58:19.702 --> 58:21.345
acres of golf course ground.

58:26.853 --> 58:29.969
Uh, that they have an interest in. The golf

58:29.985 --> 58:31.426
course is willing to sell the ground,

58:33.336 --> 58:36.504
as indicated by an email, which I inserted into your packet.

58:37.533 --> 58:41.279
And so the question is, is the council agreeable with this land transaction?

58:41.912 --> 58:45.591
If so, then we will instruct the applicant per se

58:45.687 --> 58:49.366
to go ahead and apply for a rezone, as well as a

58:49.866 --> 58:50.732
plat amendment, a minor one at that. Okay.

58:58.704 --> 58:58.817
Sorry.

59:03.664 --> 59:06.862
While he's pulling that up, does anyone have any initial thoughts

59:07.362 --> 59:10.285
that they want to share? Well, the initial question I have is, I mean,

59:10.317 --> 59:13.721
I know this borders Ranches Parkway. Is that land

59:13.866 --> 59:17.673
in any way contemplated in an expansion? And it's probably far enough

59:17.722 --> 59:18.882
off, but I just want to ask the question.

59:21.807 --> 59:23.235
I— where's Mr. Salazar?

59:25.712 --> 59:28.659
So I think you were a part of some of the conversations that we had

59:29.818 --> 59:32.958
with this particular piece of land and the right-of-way

59:33.022 --> 59:36.835
acquisition for Ranches Parkway. Based off of the image of,

59:36.883 --> 59:40.806
like, the overhead map, is any of this in risk of us needing to

59:40.902 --> 59:43.217
acquire it for our road widening projects?

59:45.359 --> 59:48.577
For the widening, no. I think we talked about using it for some

59:48.786 --> 59:52.260
public works access or utility access in

59:52.760 --> 59:53.639
this area. Okay.

59:56.304 --> 59:59.376
Any other details from staff? Not this time.

59:59.784 --> 01:00:02.848
Okay. Council, what other

01:00:02.880 --> 01:00:04.135
questions and insights do you have?

01:00:05.886 --> 01:00:09.291
So would we need to retain some kind of easement to access that

01:00:09.791 --> 01:00:10.345
property in the future?

01:00:17.580 --> 01:00:21.262
That was the only question I had. Was that question for Brad Hickman

01:00:21.682 --> 01:00:23.054
or Marcus?

01:00:27.503 --> 01:00:30.561
So right now it's golf course property, so it's subject to

01:00:31.061 --> 01:00:35.172
that agreement, which does allow us to have certain rights of

01:00:35.237 --> 01:00:37.826
access for public utilities and so on.

01:00:38.677 --> 01:00:42.091
Um, if it were sold to a private party, um,

01:00:42.703 --> 01:00:45.924
we could, as part of, uh, the reason

01:00:46.424 --> 01:00:49.529
that they're asking our permission is because there's the reversionary, or there's the clause

01:00:49.561 --> 01:00:52.826
in the, in the, in the contract, right? And,

01:00:52.923 --> 01:00:56.637
um, and so the,

01:00:57.039 --> 01:01:00.590
um, we could condition kind of

01:01:01.090 --> 01:01:04.351
releasing our interest in the property on them, uh,

01:01:04.570 --> 01:01:05.627
Grant an easement.

01:01:08.883 --> 01:01:12.345
Okay, I think— yeah, so for sure go after some sort

01:01:12.845 --> 01:01:16.387
of easement. Councilmember Wright? Yeah, I want to raise another concern.

01:01:16.435 --> 01:01:20.053
I mean, recognizing that it is part of this, this agreement, and,

01:01:20.409 --> 01:01:23.567
and not, not everyone will probably agree with me, my concern

01:01:24.067 --> 01:01:27.538
really is, um, allowing this to be sold, uh,

01:01:27.863 --> 01:01:31.663
without having something in place that indicates where

01:01:32.163 --> 01:01:35.700
those resources go, knowing that there's some, some debt that

01:01:36.200 --> 01:01:39.411
exists. Um, I, I'll probably leave

01:01:39.911 --> 01:01:42.540
it at that, and it may be something that we need to discuss in another

01:01:42.556 --> 01:01:46.093
setting, but I want at least the council to, to, to have some

01:01:47.983 --> 01:01:51.457
contemplation about that. Any other thoughts

01:01:51.957 --> 01:01:55.487
from anybody on that? Well, you know, when a, uh, I mean,

01:01:55.568 --> 01:01:58.678
in a similar circumstance like this, uh, if you sell off a piece of your

01:01:58.694 --> 01:02:02.082
land and you have a mortgage on it, The mortgage

01:02:02.130 --> 01:02:04.248
company is going to want some of that money to come pay off some of

01:02:04.748 --> 01:02:08.660
that debt. If you're in arrears. Yeah, or just even if there's

01:02:09.160 --> 01:02:12.109
a lien against any of the property, even if it's not in arrears,

01:02:12.173 --> 01:02:14.981
but even if you have a lien against the property, the mortgage company is going

01:02:15.481 --> 01:02:17.997
to require if you sell a part of it, that part of their balance comes—

01:02:18.334 --> 01:02:22.393
gets reduced as well. And maybe that's something

01:02:22.441 --> 01:02:26.019
that we're going to need to have a discussion about outside of this setting.

01:02:27.919 --> 01:02:31.207
Okay. Any other comments from staff? Any other

01:02:31.707 --> 01:02:35.341
comments from council? All right, let's move on. Our next item is going to be

01:02:35.470 --> 01:02:38.455
also introduced by Rob Hobbs. Um,

01:02:38.777 --> 01:02:41.956
and it is a discussion item about the Triumph subdivision. Now,

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:45.864
this is later in our agenda as well as a possible action,

01:02:46.623 --> 01:02:48.675
but we also have the applicant here.

01:02:49.757 --> 01:02:53.256
Do you like to start? Council,

01:02:53.723 --> 01:02:57.690
the Planning Commission advanced this to your bench

01:02:58.190 --> 01:03:01.061
with a positive recommendation upon having reviewed the matter.

01:03:02.050 --> 01:03:05.149
This is a combination of master development plan and amendment,

01:03:06.102 --> 01:03:07.506
uh, an agreement amendment.

01:03:09.307 --> 01:03:13.032
The Triumph subdivision was entitled a few

01:03:13.532 --> 01:03:17.340
years ago, and at that time was given a residential

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:21.804
Setting and zoning. The applicant is

01:03:22.304 --> 01:03:25.030
interested in, as you can see by this exhibit,

01:03:26.278 --> 01:03:29.797
creating at least 4 areas that would be for affordable housing.

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:34.999
The city has already dealt with affordable housing comments

01:03:35.128 --> 01:03:38.261
in the past, especially by staff promoting the

01:03:38.761 --> 01:03:42.602
idea. The applicant has a few asks as detailed in the staff report.

01:03:43.182 --> 01:03:46.113
To make the project, quote unquote, affordable.

01:03:48.030 --> 01:03:51.516
And they are here, as you already indicated, to explain what they

01:03:52.016 --> 01:03:56.176
would like to do. But essentially what you have are open-air

01:03:56.676 --> 01:04:02.180
tandem parking spaces with private roads built to a city structural

01:04:02.228 --> 01:04:05.523
profile, but not with the full right-of-way width, et cetera,

01:04:05.539 --> 01:04:08.268
that the city's schematics require. Okay.

01:04:09.294 --> 01:04:12.749
And then you have a select set. They had 3 samples shown to

01:04:13.249 --> 01:04:16.525
the commission of possible house designs that would work

01:04:17.025 --> 01:04:21.279
for each of the lots. We would have reduced lot sizes and expected

01:04:21.779 --> 01:04:24.816
setbacks as well. And that would make it, uh,

01:04:24.946 --> 01:04:29.030
for them pencil out better. They think that'd be an introductory

01:04:29.063 --> 01:04:32.290
comment. Any questions? Any questions, counsel?

01:04:33.338 --> 01:04:36.230
All right, can we invite the applicant to come up?

01:04:37.110 --> 01:04:38.430
And field some questions.

01:04:41.390 --> 01:04:45.367
Please introduce yourself. Are we

01:04:45.867 --> 01:04:49.546
on? There we go. Scott Hazard. Thanks for your time today. What questions can

01:04:49.626 --> 01:04:52.761
I answer for you? Council, what questions do we

01:04:53.261 --> 01:04:53.757
have for this applicant?

01:04:59.550 --> 01:05:04.622
I'll jump in. I mean, I got a lot of different notes, but Okay.

01:05:04.686 --> 01:05:08.562
So, oh man, I got a few questions. I like, I love the affordable housing

01:05:08.755 --> 01:05:12.036
purchase price cap because that sort of

01:05:12.536 --> 01:05:15.010
solves one of the issues I was contemplating a few days ago is that if

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:18.963
you have a family size of 8, they could, their 80% of what they could

01:05:19.463 --> 01:05:22.305
buy, the purchase price would be much different than a family size of 1.

01:05:23.204 --> 01:05:25.586
Then someone could buy in as family size is 1 and then turn around and

01:05:26.086 --> 01:05:27.662
flip it to a family size of 8 and make a profit. But that's,

01:05:27.679 --> 01:05:31.659
so I like the introduction of the cap. Based on the numbers we just saw

01:05:32.192 --> 01:05:35.661
from the CDBG

01:05:36.161 --> 01:05:40.185
presentation, it looked like the 80% AMI

01:05:40.284 --> 01:05:43.936
of a 2-household was $76,300. If you

01:05:44.436 --> 01:05:47.801
do a multiplier of 3.25, that puts you at a purchase price just under $310,000.

01:05:50.073 --> 01:05:53.762
So that sounds good. I mean, is that sort of what you're anticipating?

01:05:53.811 --> 01:05:58.610
That's at 80% AMI. We will not be below $310,000.

01:05:58.640 --> 01:06:02.360
So our starting homes will be probably more like $325,000,

01:06:02.860 --> 01:06:06.160
$329,000. Okay. So that multiplier— so that multiplier,

01:06:06.660 --> 01:06:08.820
I think, caps where you would be. So in other words, that multiplier would be

01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:12.440
below— we would need to increase that multiplier in order to

01:06:12.940 --> 01:06:16.120
go to $325,000 because otherwise our current multiplier is hitting us

01:06:16.620 --> 01:06:19.800
at $309,968 for a max purchase price. You're talking about for the deed restriction?

01:06:21.503 --> 01:06:24.808
So, the, the multiplier sets in

01:06:24.856 --> 01:06:27.904
after the first sale. After the first sale. Yeah.

01:06:27.984 --> 01:06:31.898
And the intent is exactly what you just described, where we didn't want

01:06:31.994 --> 01:06:35.668
to just let an equity grab happen because as developers, we're leaving

01:06:35.684 --> 01:06:38.924
a ton of money on the table to make this work. Um,

01:06:39.309 --> 01:06:42.758
and we're all— obviously, there's also a lot that the city's contributing to try to

01:06:43.258 --> 01:06:45.790
make this work, at least that I'm asking you to. So, we didn't, we didn't

01:06:45.806 --> 01:06:49.384
want gen 1 of buyer to just benefit and then

01:06:49.416 --> 01:06:51.986
take a, take a bunch of money and bail. So,

01:06:52.564 --> 01:06:57.029
so we've created that restriction as a multiplier so that purchase

01:06:57.529 --> 01:07:02.457
price will always scale with AMI. So it's—

01:07:02.505 --> 01:07:05.797
we felt it was better, and we're open to feedback.

01:07:06.360 --> 01:07:10.439
We felt it was better than putting a 3% or 3.5% or 4% annual

01:07:10.939 --> 01:07:14.679
cap to the purchase price because it allows them to grow equity as

01:07:15.067 --> 01:07:17.998
income increases on the average within the state.

01:07:19.536 --> 01:07:23.229
And it's always tied— the idea is to service that population.

01:07:23.940 --> 01:07:27.617
And so equity gain is basically just tied to income.

01:07:29.733 --> 01:07:34.277
Yeah. Yeah, I love this idea. I mean, we all know that

01:07:34.777 --> 01:07:37.754
the median income has not kept up with the median home price in Utah.

01:07:38.207 --> 01:07:41.942
Yeah, this attempts to solve that problem. It's deeded.

01:07:42.230 --> 01:07:45.565
So there's, like you said, you can't buy the property

01:07:46.065 --> 01:07:48.707
and then flip it a year later to a higher price. It's always just going

01:07:49.207 --> 01:07:51.641
to follow the median income, which is going to help. I think I'm just thinking

01:07:51.690 --> 01:07:54.880
of a lot of young starter families trying to get into a home.

01:07:55.056 --> 01:07:58.295
It's innovative. Yeah, that the deed restriction was

01:07:58.391 --> 01:08:01.614
probably our biggest challenge to bring this back to you in a formal format to

01:08:01.758 --> 01:08:05.590
ask for a vote because of those types of challenges and then

01:08:05.622 --> 01:08:09.310
getting appraisers and underwriters to sing out of our songbook so they could be Freddie

01:08:09.810 --> 01:08:13.589
Fannie underwritten. They can't be HUD-funded, so we can't do an FHA/VA

01:08:13.718 --> 01:08:17.019
loan. They won't allow the deed restriction. But there's been a lot

01:08:17.519 --> 01:08:20.724
of collaboration and thought with many different parties to

01:08:20.740 --> 01:08:23.912
try to get this to a point where we can get loans on it,

01:08:23.945 --> 01:08:27.030
we can get it funded. And our intent is that these are,

01:08:27.111 --> 01:08:30.976
these are designed to be attainable in perpetuity. All other programs

01:08:31.476 --> 01:08:33.644
that I'm aware of in the state cap at like 10 years, and then it's

01:08:34.144 --> 01:08:37.324
free-for-all, do whatever you want. I'm like, why did we spend all this time and

01:08:37.389 --> 01:08:41.136
effort? for a 10-year program. So tying it to

01:08:41.636 --> 01:08:45.111
the AMI, I think, should allow us to, to run in perpetuity.

01:08:45.626 --> 01:08:48.946
And then in the deed restriction, we've empowered the council

01:08:50.365 --> 01:08:53.815
to make modifications to that down the road if it seems like it makes sense.

01:08:54.382 --> 01:08:58.063
We've created some restrictions there because we didn't want you pestered with every little—

01:08:58.902 --> 01:09:01.065
everybody wants something. It has to be a market-wide,

01:09:01.648 --> 01:09:05.368
industry-wide impact, a community-wide impact. So that you're

01:09:05.868 --> 01:09:09.269
not hearing requests from every individual little owner to say, hey, make an exception for

01:09:09.769 --> 01:09:13.320
me. Can you change this? Uh, because we recognize 10 or 15, 20,

01:09:13.497 --> 01:09:17.362
30 years down the road, the dynamic might be different. Maybe income doesn't

01:09:17.394 --> 01:09:20.759
scale nearly as much as it needs to, and there needs to be

01:09:21.259 --> 01:09:25.219
some concession. The multiplier might need to change. Uh, so we've empowered

01:09:25.719 --> 01:09:29.761
the council to be able to make those decisions. Go for

01:09:29.859 --> 01:09:32.944
it. Just one other. Yeah, I have a lot of questions building in my head.

01:09:33.843 --> 01:09:37.486
This, these, these pods will be under an HOA.

01:09:37.871 --> 01:09:41.674
Yes. Is that correct? Yes. Same HOA as the properties outside of the

01:09:41.899 --> 01:09:45.654
whole— Yes, they'll be like an HOA that'll handle just

01:09:46.154 --> 01:09:49.489
these attainable housing clusters. But yes. Okay. So,

01:09:49.505 --> 01:09:52.843
so maybe this section needs to be clarified because

01:09:52.859 --> 01:09:55.523
when I'm looking at the affordable purchase price cap,

01:09:56.406 --> 01:09:58.059
one of the sections pertaining to that says,

01:09:59.437 --> 01:10:02.721
that any transfer shall be

01:10:02.818 --> 01:10:05.830
equal to the product of the resale multiple and the AMI.

01:10:05.926 --> 01:10:09.194
And it says any transfer. So I didn't realize— so

01:10:09.210 --> 01:10:12.671
when I'm reading that, I was thinking when you're selling it as well, because I

01:10:12.735 --> 01:10:15.955
would assume that would be a transfer unless that was defined otherwise within the contract,

01:10:16.195 --> 01:10:19.736
within this language here. Colby, do you have a better response

01:10:20.236 --> 01:10:22.620
to that, or Kaden? So you're saying for the initial sale? Well,

01:10:23.421 --> 01:10:26.850
so, yeah, what Mr. Hazard is saying is the initial sale would not be subject

01:10:27.350 --> 01:10:30.490
to the resale multiplier. But I didn't— that wasn't clear to me in the,

01:10:30.843 --> 01:10:34.132
in the deed restriction language. If I understand your

01:10:34.632 --> 01:10:38.127
question, that 3.25 is a plug for the time being.

01:10:38.625 --> 01:10:42.187
When we go to sell these homes in these communities, we will set the multiplier

01:10:42.235 --> 01:10:45.460
to equal the purchase price. That makes sense. So that when,

01:10:45.941 --> 01:10:49.230
when I'm a new buyer and I purchase the home, the purchase price, if I

01:10:49.730 --> 01:10:53.418
take my area median income then and times it by the

01:10:53.918 --> 01:10:56.290
multiplier in the deed restriction, it will equal the purchase price that I bought it

01:10:56.354 --> 01:10:59.721
for. So we will sell it at the qualifying purchase price and

01:11:00.221 --> 01:11:04.178
then from there, AMI will increase their purchase price in the future. So that multiplier

01:11:04.210 --> 01:11:07.304
is going to change with every single contract? No. So we plan to do it

01:11:07.336 --> 01:11:10.606
on a node-per-node basis because of the cost of construction.

01:11:11.375 --> 01:11:14.517
So when we build out phase 2, like the first node here,

01:11:14.950 --> 01:11:18.140
we'll have set purchase prices for each floor plan there. And we might have,

01:11:18.397 --> 01:11:21.795
you know, 3 or 4 floor plans, but we intend to build them

01:11:22.003 --> 01:11:26.866
all out at once. And to get some economies of scale there to further

01:11:27.366 --> 01:11:31.334
contribute to the affordability aspect. But so each node will have its separate

01:11:31.788 --> 01:11:33.018
multiplier, essentially.

01:11:36.013 --> 01:11:39.315
Any other thoughts? Now, this is an item that shows up later

01:11:39.331 --> 01:11:43.418
in the agenda for a possible action. Are there any details of

01:11:43.450 --> 01:11:46.452
that item that we need to hammer out? Yeah, so I've got a few more

01:11:46.952 --> 01:11:50.219
questions. So the, so the one of the asks Is that the

01:11:50.719 --> 01:11:53.530
city provide or purchase the water necessary?

01:11:55.140 --> 01:11:57.774
I would like, I would like that quantified in terms of like, how much are

01:11:58.274 --> 01:12:01.086
we asking? And I think it would be coming from RDA dollars.

01:12:02.447 --> 01:12:06.251
How much is in our RDA to actually provide? So that's actually

01:12:06.267 --> 01:12:10.071
something I wanted to make a note on. It's a good question.

01:12:10.684 --> 01:12:13.993
I think that we need to make it tied to available RDA

01:12:14.493 --> 01:12:18.206
funds. So, if that runs out at a future date, that we're

01:12:18.706 --> 01:12:21.138
not now taking that out of our general fund to buy water for these.

01:12:21.875 --> 01:12:25.096
So, I think that that probably needs to be some language that's put in there

01:12:25.596 --> 01:12:29.309
that if the RDA funds are no longer available. And then

01:12:29.341 --> 01:12:32.786
the other thing too is I just wanted to be clear, market water that

01:12:33.286 --> 01:12:35.718
is on the market, but we're not providing that from our own stores.

01:12:36.135 --> 01:12:39.579
Yeah, the proposal has never been for the city to provide

01:12:39.595 --> 01:12:43.184
water or to sell water. That's never been the proposal. In fact, I think

01:12:43.684 --> 01:12:47.066
it came from Uh, this body, I was the one who suggested it.

01:12:47.115 --> 01:12:50.613
I'll take the blame. Yeah. Uh, the idea that maybe RDA

01:12:50.725 --> 01:12:53.887
funds could be used to purchase this on the open market. There's 2 variables there

01:12:53.951 --> 01:12:56.888
that I think are important for us to define, or at least create a methodology.

01:12:57.626 --> 01:13:01.012
Uh, one is we need staff to calculate what's our,

01:13:01.959 --> 01:13:05.537
you know, per acre foot, what's our quantity requirement for these small

01:13:05.570 --> 01:13:09.806
houses on really small yards. Uh, my sense is it's probably different

01:13:09.822 --> 01:13:12.968
than what's in the in the existing table because it's a very different

01:13:13.468 --> 01:13:17.382
product type. Uh, minimal outdoor irrigation, very water-wise landscaping,

01:13:17.398 --> 01:13:20.736
and very small homes. So that would be one factor

01:13:21.236 --> 01:13:23.304
that we would need to determine. The other thing that I think we should insert,

01:13:23.320 --> 01:13:26.242
and I've had this conversation with staff but didn't know what number to put in,

01:13:26.354 --> 01:13:29.917
is that there probably needs to be a cap on a per acre-foot

01:13:30.417 --> 01:13:34.331
number. Um, you know, so I, I don't call

01:13:34.831 --> 01:13:38.713
my buddy Isaac and say, hey, sell me some water at $65,000 an acre-foot,

01:13:38.729 --> 01:13:41.364
and then the city's on the hook. To pay for it. So I think we

01:13:41.864 --> 01:13:45.881
ought to have a methodology or insert a number, something that's reasonably representative of

01:13:46.381 --> 01:13:50.730
market value that's still attainable, where we could actually acquire

01:13:50.876 --> 01:13:54.523
water at that number. But that's reflective of a market rate. What if we

01:13:54.571 --> 01:13:57.777
tied it to what our ability to sell it, what the price,

01:13:58.261 --> 01:14:01.662
if we were to use our own stores, we would contribute

01:14:01.742 --> 01:14:05.609
up to that amount of money, and then the responsibility for the remaining amount

01:14:06.109 --> 01:14:08.970
would be up to the developer. That would just add to the purchase price.

01:14:09.410 --> 01:14:12.750
So, I mean, if you think about it right now, right now, it would be

01:14:13.250 --> 01:14:16.330
roughly 10% of the cost of the home. What do you get that right now?

01:14:16.680 --> 01:14:20.390
$31,000 for an acre foot of water?

01:14:20.890 --> 01:14:24.240
Just— yeah, I've seen it sell for as high as $35,000.

01:14:24.510 --> 01:14:27.670
So I've been— we'll use $28,000 to $32,000. Say $31,000,

01:14:28.170 --> 01:14:31.490
right in the middle. I think right now it's selling, uh, if we sell seed

01:14:32.090 --> 01:14:35.520
CDB water, I think it's $21,000, $22,000.

01:14:36.020 --> 01:14:40.100
So, yeah, $21,000 responsible for $9,000 an acre-foot.

01:14:40.180 --> 01:14:43.640
You're not going to hit a full acre-foot for a house that size.

01:14:44.660 --> 01:14:48.140
So, I think it's reasonable, but that's probably

01:14:48.640 --> 01:14:52.140
the only way you're going to be able to follow that in a reasonable way.

01:14:52.640 --> 01:14:56.740
Has to be tied to something that's fixed. Yeah, that's an amount that is fixed.

01:14:57.280 --> 01:15:01.160
The only— I think that that creates some safety for the city. The only challenge

01:15:01.660 --> 01:15:05.810
I see with that, Rich, is You know, water is becoming an increasingly scarce

01:15:06.310 --> 01:15:09.450
resource. And if water does jump to $40,000,

01:15:09.950 --> 01:15:13.930
$50,000, $60,000 an acre-foot, I can't deliver these. There's just no way I can

01:15:14.430 --> 01:15:17.290
subsidize that kind of money to make— to hit these price points and make it

01:15:17.790 --> 01:15:20.310
happen. Or we have to add it into the price of the home, and then

01:15:20.810 --> 01:15:23.730
we've thrown ourselves out of that 80-90 AMI target, which is what we're after.

01:15:25.270 --> 01:15:28.340
I think the suggestion that I would make would be,

01:15:28.510 --> 01:15:31.844
Scott, I mean, recognizing that that there's some variables

01:15:31.860 --> 01:15:34.286
that need to be worked out. I think if we can put together just some

01:15:34.786 --> 01:15:38.046
brackets that, that, that you're comfortable with and the city becomes comfortable

01:15:38.546 --> 01:15:42.046
with, that makes a lot of sense to me. The other thing is, you know,

01:15:42.062 --> 01:15:45.982
I think Councilmember Wood makes a good point, is, is subject to the fact that

01:15:45.998 --> 01:15:49.629
there are available RDA dollars to purchase the water. But I think

01:15:50.129 --> 01:15:52.810
we should just look at it pod by pod. And, and I know that you,

01:15:53.148 --> 01:15:56.264
you know, with the concept that you have in place, you're saying,

01:15:56.457 --> 01:15:59.491
look, I just want to trans— I just want to transfer this concept to the

01:15:59.991 --> 01:16:03.215
next area, to the next area, with some subtle, perhaps subtle design changes.

01:16:03.279 --> 01:16:06.730
I don't know. One of the things that I want to make sure that

01:16:06.794 --> 01:16:10.518
we're really focused on— you're saying that you might have 3 or 4 different plans,

01:16:11.063 --> 01:16:14.321
and you've shown us some really beautiful designs, at least in my opinion,

01:16:14.819 --> 01:16:18.639
that make a lot of sense. I think they're super livable. I think we need

01:16:19.139 --> 01:16:21.752
to have the language in there that recognizes you're going to put the refrigerator and

01:16:22.252 --> 01:16:24.062
the stove and all those things that we've talked about. out in there. I,

01:16:24.223 --> 01:16:27.283
I don't know that that was in there. I didn't see it. It probably is

01:16:27.783 --> 01:16:30.696
somewhere and I just missed it. Um, so those are a couple of the things

01:16:31.196 --> 01:16:34.382
that I'd just like to see get changed, but recognize that this first pot is

01:16:34.398 --> 01:16:38.580
really an experiment. And, and I don't— look,

01:16:38.644 --> 01:16:41.913
I think, I, I think it needs to be said publicly that you're being pretty

01:16:41.929 --> 01:16:45.021
generous in coming to the table and trying to help us develop this.

01:16:45.758 --> 01:16:48.755
I'm really grateful for that, and we're trying to,

01:16:48.963 --> 01:16:52.412
to, to help you get over some of those hurdles that you're facing to be

01:16:52.912 --> 01:16:56.122
able to deliver a product that's pretty unique. And my goal and our hope,

01:16:56.186 --> 01:16:59.607
I think, is if we can't set a standard by

01:16:59.639 --> 01:17:02.482
which the rest of the state can learn something, and, uh,

01:17:03.012 --> 01:17:06.385
and maybe we can actually start to tackle the real problem

01:17:06.885 --> 01:17:09.179
with affordable housing and get our kids into single-family homes.

01:17:09.758 --> 01:17:13.227
Yeah, and that's my real goal, Brett. This is not a one-off thing for

01:17:13.727 --> 01:17:17.354
me. Uh, I, I see Eagle Mountain leading out with a product like this

01:17:17.563 --> 01:17:20.665
to show the rest of the state on what can happen When a willing

01:17:20.682 --> 01:17:23.726
city and a willing developer can collaborate and come up with some solutions.

01:17:24.145 --> 01:17:26.577
And I'm sure there's plenty of warts we're going to shave off of this first

01:17:27.077 --> 01:17:29.107
cluster and then figure out how to do it better in the next one.

01:17:29.640 --> 01:17:34.391
The purchase price inclusions that you referenced are not included in the amendment

01:17:34.891 --> 01:17:37.478
to the development agreement, and we can incorporate those. I don't have any issue with

01:17:37.978 --> 01:17:41.527
that. I just— it's funny when you're driving through

01:17:42.027 --> 01:17:45.458
other cities and you see $600,000 townhomes as affordable housing.

01:17:45.507 --> 01:17:48.729
I'm like, that's This, this really is trying to solve

01:17:48.745 --> 01:17:52.680
the problem, and I'm excited about it. The other thing I'm excited about is none

01:17:53.180 --> 01:17:56.679
of these properties can be rented out. They will all be primary owned,

01:17:57.257 --> 01:18:00.373
which is awesome. The enforcement of that's going to be challenging.

01:18:00.469 --> 01:18:03.264
Right now we're saddling the HOA with that. They're kind of acting as,

01:18:03.842 --> 01:18:06.797
as Rob put in the staff report, as a de facto housing authority.

01:18:07.456 --> 01:18:11.535
But we'll do what we can as the city matures

01:18:11.583 --> 01:18:14.298
and develops, and maybe at some point you've got a housing authority and we can

01:18:14.798 --> 01:18:17.871
turn that element over to the city, or maybe we just— I have full confidence

01:18:18.371 --> 01:18:20.060
in our HOA Karens to take care of that.

01:18:27.859 --> 01:18:31.044
So, so the HOA will take care of the roads?

01:18:32.208 --> 01:18:35.603
No. So the only public infrastructure element we've asked

01:18:35.911 --> 01:18:39.422
the city to handle are roads and utilities. All of the surface

01:18:39.487 --> 01:18:42.424
landscape improvements would be handled by the HOA, including irrigation.

01:18:43.467 --> 01:18:47.509
Uh, but maintenance of the utilities in the road and the road itself would

01:18:47.557 --> 01:18:51.534
be, uh, would be the city's responsibility. So snow removal,

01:18:51.582 --> 01:18:54.950
crack seal, you know, asphalt repair, all of

01:18:55.450 --> 01:18:58.510
that would be under the city like you would any other road. Okay, and then

01:18:58.558 --> 01:19:01.766
the other, the other, other questions. But so

01:19:02.266 --> 01:19:06.144
parking, because then the roads are so narrow, I assume that you're restricting parking

01:19:07.122 --> 01:19:10.699
so it cannot be on the street. Yes, but I didn't see that verbiage in

01:19:10.731 --> 01:19:13.891
the CC&amp;Rs. There's a section on parking that didn't seem clear to me.

01:19:13.939 --> 01:19:16.907
11.6. Maybe I wasn't reading it. Yeah,

01:19:16.987 --> 01:19:20.034
we would add a note. We would add a note to the amended plat for

01:19:20.534 --> 01:19:23.082
that, and we've worked that out in DRC with the fire chief. I didn't realize

01:19:23.582 --> 01:19:26.739
this, but a red curb is insufficient. You need signage. So we'll, we'll,

01:19:27.012 --> 01:19:31.295
we'll work on that. But yes, no, no parking on the street

01:19:31.795 --> 01:19:35.866
side other than the little razor strips of parking that we've added for additional overflow

01:19:36.366 --> 01:19:40.073
and visitor parking. Just for reference, will you just once again

01:19:40.105 --> 01:19:43.477
tell us a 2-bedroom is going to sell at— what are you calculating it at

01:19:43.977 --> 01:19:47.283
currently? In the low $300s, um, probably $330-ish,

01:19:47.958 --> 01:19:51.748
um, for, for 2-bed, 1.5-bath. Uh,

01:19:51.828 --> 01:19:54.976
the biggest plan, which is our 2-story plan, uh, we priced out

01:19:55.024 --> 01:19:59.424
last at about $374,000, and that includes all appliances.

01:19:59.537 --> 01:20:02.893
So that's fridge, washer, and dryer included, includes all the window

01:20:03.393 --> 01:20:06.827
coverings. It includes all landscaping front and back

01:20:06.907 --> 01:20:10.360
and all the fencing. So all of those things that are really a first cost

01:20:10.440 --> 01:20:13.732
outlay for a new homebuyer when they buy a home, then they realize, oh my

01:20:14.232 --> 01:20:16.702
goodness, I have all these other expenses. The only thing they really have to worry

01:20:17.202 --> 01:20:20.540
about is furnishing the inside. Everything else is covered in the— And my last question,

01:20:20.572 --> 01:20:23.911
Scott, and it's really a compliment to you. Is there anybody in the audience that

01:20:24.265 --> 01:20:26.721
knows somebody that would benefit or purchase one of these?

01:20:29.549 --> 01:20:33.398
I just think this is— I think this is an amazing opportunity to

01:20:33.415 --> 01:20:36.717
try something different. Thank you. Oh,

01:20:37.055 --> 01:20:40.539
Councilmember Wood, did you have just thoughts? I'm sure we're going to talk some more

01:20:40.845 --> 01:20:44.719
about this. I'm not firm on how we

01:20:45.219 --> 01:20:48.661
deal with the water, but I want to— I mean, you know,

01:20:48.694 --> 01:20:53.034
I'm a workshop. Let's talk about it. Let's figure out something that is scalable,

01:20:53.536 --> 01:20:56.772
that doesn't require a lot of oversight. Um,

01:20:57.144 --> 01:20:59.405
one of the things I was— when I was looking through the deed restriction,

01:20:59.485 --> 01:21:04.798
I am concerned about the city

01:21:05.218 --> 01:21:08.173
being required to deal with all the transfer stuff. I would love to see that

01:21:08.221 --> 01:21:10.481
be a 3rd party, frankly,

01:21:12.257 --> 01:21:16.018
just because that can— things can change and sway and

01:21:16.131 --> 01:21:19.457
whatever. And if we want this to have lasting, it needs to be

01:21:20.829 --> 01:21:25.183
someone that is actually Running this program, maybe it's a 501

01:21:25.683 --> 01:21:28.935
or something like that, that is responsible for doing this, that doesn't have skin in

01:21:28.967 --> 01:21:32.976
the game. And I don't know what that looks like, but I really

01:21:33.040 --> 01:21:37.755
want this to last. I think, you know, that I'm invested in, in this working.

01:21:38.413 --> 01:21:42.326
I believe that this is the only attainable housing product

01:21:42.502 --> 01:21:45.982
in the state. Everything else that I have seen develop

01:21:46.431 --> 01:21:49.860
is something that is not really producing the outcomes that we're

01:21:50.360 --> 01:21:53.907
looking for, for the individual that's buying the house. And I,

01:21:54.375 --> 01:21:56.876
I'm proud of it. I think we put a lot of work into this one.

01:21:56.940 --> 01:21:59.926
I want it to work, but I want it to be something that's scalable that

01:22:00.426 --> 01:22:02.927
we can do throughout the city. I hope other people steal it and want to

01:22:02.960 --> 01:22:06.268
do it in the rest of the state, because I think it's a good program.

01:22:06.946 --> 01:22:09.931
Councilmember Wood, I'm just going to make a quick suggestion. I mean, we already have

01:22:10.431 --> 01:22:15.755
someone reviewing all of our, all of our loan applications for those who are

01:22:16.255 --> 01:22:18.140
Or seeking the forgivable loan that comes out of those RDA funds. I just think

01:22:18.640 --> 01:22:21.687
that the city could help participate in reviewing that at some point in time.

01:22:22.634 --> 01:22:26.022
That's a great suggestion because it's all of her underwriters that we've vetted this through.

01:22:27.001 --> 01:22:30.228
So she seemed like the,

01:22:30.356 --> 01:22:34.048
the perfect one to manage that end of the process. And so she's

01:22:34.548 --> 01:22:38.222
been included all along. So I think all the points and questions

01:22:38.722 --> 01:22:40.855
I heard are very valid. It looks like we've got a few things to sort

01:22:41.355 --> 01:22:45.926
out. amend, uh, before we're ready to go to a vote. Is that a

01:22:46.426 --> 01:22:49.024
fair stand— fair statement? I think we can probably deal with most of these things

01:22:49.200 --> 01:22:52.747
in a— in a motion. In a motion, don't you think? I mean, so I

01:22:53.247 --> 01:22:55.924
think when, when we come back, we've addressed most of the concerns.

01:22:56.984 --> 01:23:00.530
Um, having an administrator do that— Marcus, if you think that we need

01:23:01.030 --> 01:23:04.655
to have more strong language than what we've talked about,

01:23:04.719 --> 01:23:07.367
tell us now, and we can give them the ability to go and do that.

01:23:07.906 --> 01:23:11.368
But I think I don't want to sit on this much longer.

01:23:13.100 --> 01:23:16.024
The prices of houses go up as we continue to sit. So,

01:23:16.088 --> 01:23:19.462
just so you know, my first phase of Triumph is submitted to staff for

01:23:19.962 --> 01:23:23.403
final plat, does not include this, but there's a slight adjustment I'll

01:23:23.903 --> 01:23:27.475
need to make in the first phase to accommodate this. And I'm hoping to knock

01:23:27.539 --> 01:23:30.933
through this right away so that I can start delivering houses

01:23:31.433 --> 01:23:32.772
next, next spring, summer.

01:23:35.046 --> 01:23:39.523
One, one. Oh, sorry. Hang on. I want to make sure Marcus answers my question.

01:23:40.860 --> 01:23:44.130
You know, I won't have any time during this meeting to hammer out the

01:23:44.630 --> 01:23:47.865
language, but I'm sure we could figure

01:23:48.365 --> 01:23:51.567
it out. Watch the recording, make sure we encompass everything that's in

01:23:51.600 --> 01:23:54.992
there and work through it. That's what the council wants

01:23:59.134 --> 01:24:02.490
One thing that's kind of a sticking point for me,

01:24:02.506 --> 01:24:06.184
and I'd love to, like you said, workshop this— the water price.

01:24:06.907 --> 01:24:10.569
I know that we have to find a target, we have to

01:24:10.617 --> 01:24:14.439
find a fixed number. Sure. But I also don't want to automatically

01:24:14.471 --> 01:24:19.033
basically underfund the water by like $9,000

01:24:19.049 --> 01:24:22.791
per acre-foot. So I would ask that we look at

01:24:22.983 --> 01:24:26.625
maybe what market is going for right now. And set the cap

01:24:27.191 --> 01:24:31.011
similar to that, as opposed to the Central Utah Water

01:24:31.205 --> 01:24:34.672
Project water, because we all know that no one's touching

01:24:35.355 --> 01:24:38.719
residential water at that $21 or whatever price it is right

01:24:38.752 --> 01:24:42.820
now for us. Yeah, it was just a suggestion.

01:24:43.029 --> 01:24:46.348
I think there probably needs to be some sort of a scale. Maybe even

01:24:47.041 --> 01:24:50.425
we create some parameters based on, you know,

01:24:50.522 --> 01:24:53.372
sales price or Size of lot or whatever.

01:24:53.662 --> 01:24:56.567
I think that's one of those moving things. We got to get that part figured

01:24:57.067 --> 01:25:01.006
out, but I'm open to suggestions for sure. Yeah, as am I. And I'd still

01:25:01.506 --> 01:25:05.197
like— because I don't feel like we have the data on how much

01:25:05.697 --> 01:25:08.663
money do we have in the RDA funds? How much money is going to be

01:25:09.163 --> 01:25:12.421
required? Like, I don't have that data. Based on that, I would like to see

01:25:12.921 --> 01:25:16.132
those numbers. Staff can— staff. Yeah, Evan, you probably could shoot that from the

01:25:16.632 --> 01:25:20.747
hip and just tell us. Wasn't it $1.7

01:25:21.247 --> 01:25:25.284
million last year was what went into the fund, and that was with a fraction?

01:25:26.680 --> 01:25:29.705
Will you say that one more time? And we'll— okay,

01:25:30.368 --> 01:25:33.652
so, and, and how many

01:25:34.152 --> 01:25:37.253
shares would be required for this project? I'd need

01:25:37.753 --> 01:25:40.883
staff to do a calculation for me on that, but my assessment is it would

01:25:41.383 --> 01:25:44.930
be on, on a market value per per acre-foot, it's probably somewhere

01:25:45.430 --> 01:25:48.660
in the $20,000 to $25,000 per unit.

01:25:49.190 --> 01:25:53.260
And how many units are contemplated? There's 32 in this first phase,

01:25:53.760 --> 01:25:56.250
and they're all about— they're, they're all a little smaller, a little bigger. But so

01:25:56.750 --> 01:26:00.210
30, 32, um, okay,

01:26:00.710 --> 01:26:04.790
times $25,000, so $800,000 per pod.

01:26:05.290 --> 01:26:08.790
But, but each pod's different than— like, some have more than 32

01:26:09.290 --> 01:26:12.670
or less than 32 on these different pods. But tonight's, tonight's discussion would

01:26:13.170 --> 01:26:14.952
be inclusive of all the pods, correct?

01:26:16.092 --> 01:26:20.185
Or are we only looking at 1 pod with the agreement right

01:26:20.685 --> 01:26:23.749
now? I mean, my, my, my, I would sort of favor, given how new this

01:26:23.878 --> 01:26:27.827
is, to approve pod by pod, just in, or even pod 1.

01:26:27.891 --> 01:26:30.059
Let's see how it goes. And we're like, yeah, this is so good. We're approving

01:26:30.559 --> 01:26:33.623
these other, other 3. Yes. And I think your

01:26:33.751 --> 01:26:37.363
point is well taken, Councilmember Whiting. I think, I think that what I would probably

01:26:37.379 --> 01:26:40.397
suggest is that we enter into this agreement Pod 1,

01:26:41.038 --> 01:26:44.662
recognizing and perhaps entitling 2, 3, and 4 for

01:26:45.162 --> 01:26:48.447
a similar or a like outcome, but realizing that it's got to come back

01:26:48.511 --> 01:26:51.622
so that we can tweak those details that are going to pertain to each

01:26:51.638 --> 01:26:55.518
pod. Well, and if we're going to consider

01:26:55.951 --> 01:26:59.463
using RDA funds for this, I mean, that's a big chunk of money we're doing.

01:26:59.719 --> 01:27:02.830
And anyway, that's where I think is we got to sort of

01:27:02.878 --> 01:27:06.454
consider this pod by pod. I think we all kind of are good

01:27:06.954 --> 01:27:10.721
with that. So, could we instruct staff in the motion that

01:27:11.221 --> 01:27:14.469
this applies to Pod 1, and then we'll come back for the rest of them?

01:27:14.662 --> 01:27:18.036
I mean, what do we need to do to get this across the finish line?

01:27:19.876 --> 01:27:22.897
Are you okay with Pod 1 being approved? Yeah, yeah, I,

01:27:23.171 --> 01:27:26.144
I just, I would want to see this successful, and I think this is,

01:27:26.725 --> 01:27:29.903
this is going to require collaboration as we go.

01:27:30.934 --> 01:27:34.045
Um, so, you know, my intent is we get through Pod 1,

01:27:34.303 --> 01:27:37.770
I'm going to come back to you, even if it were Already vested and titled.

01:27:37.850 --> 01:27:40.718
I'm going to come back to you to return a report and say, this is

01:27:40.782 --> 01:27:43.922
what went well, and these are targets we totally missed, and this is how we're

01:27:44.422 --> 01:27:47.528
adjusting for the next phase. So, I see this

01:27:48.028 --> 01:27:51.053
as an ongoing collaborative effort, and you're on the journey with me regardless. I think

01:27:51.553 --> 01:27:54.578
we're in the saddle together. And if the biggest issue really is just the water

01:27:55.078 --> 01:27:59.272
right issue, you know, we could say, okay, generally we're looking at these 4 clusters

01:27:59.385 --> 01:28:03.518
or those 4 pods, but water rights will address on a phase-by-phase

01:28:03.615 --> 01:28:07.226
to assess What's in your budget? How much is a prevailing cost

01:28:07.726 --> 01:28:10.758
to buy the water? Um, you know,

01:28:10.838 --> 01:28:14.194
maybe that's something that we look at at a phase-by-phase to see if there's funding

01:28:14.226 --> 01:28:18.722
for it, and maybe we wait. Maybe we don't deliver that cluster in

01:28:19.222 --> 01:28:22.190
that timing, and the timing, uh, you know, runs with the funding.

01:28:22.960 --> 01:28:25.963
So one more thing to that, though. I think it's important to remember,

01:28:26.493 --> 01:28:30.429
even if it had to add an additional $10,000 to it because of the

01:28:30.929 --> 01:28:34.215
economy of whatever it is at the time. These are cheap.

01:28:35.850 --> 01:28:39.342
I mean, I didn't think that's being lost on people what this affordability is actually

01:28:39.846 --> 01:28:43.810
creating that does not exist. So, as we're haggling over

01:28:44.310 --> 01:28:47.611
$1,000 or $10,000, recognize that

01:28:48.371 --> 01:28:51.897
they are not buying an appliance package. They're not doing

01:28:52.075 --> 01:28:54.739
any of their own landscaping,

01:28:55.325 --> 01:28:58.567
fencing, Those are tremendous costs that people are

01:28:58.680 --> 01:29:01.459
going to have to do with any other product. So,

01:29:01.669 --> 01:29:03.236
even if we get close, we won.

01:29:05.160 --> 01:29:08.485
Yeah, I agree. And there may be different ways to

01:29:08.985 --> 01:29:12.855
help with the offset of the funding. Water just seemed like an element that

01:29:12.904 --> 01:29:16.130
we could address directly because it's such a big component. But like on

01:29:16.630 --> 01:29:19.084
